Haiku kernel: an mmap() can panic the machine through _RandomizeAddress().

    PANIC: ASSERT FAILED (src/system/kernel/vm/VMUserAddressSpace.cpp:465):
           start <= end
    Thread 610 "NetworkService" running on CPU 1

      panic
      VMUserAddressSpace::_RandomizeAddress(...)
      VMUserAddressSpace::_InsertAreaSlot(0x100000, 0x80000 (524288),
          0x7fffffff, 0x6 (6), 0x1000 (4096), VMUserArea*, 0x3)
      VMUserAddressSpace::InsertArea(...)
      vm_map_cache(..., "content_shell mmap area", 0x80000 (524288), ...)
      _vm_map_file(6, ...)
      _user_map_file
      handle_syscall

Seen twice on hrev99002+148 (x86_gcc2, 2 GB machine) while Chromium 114
loaded x.com. A userland mmap() should not be able to take the kernel down,
whatever state the address space is in.

## Reading the arguments

_InsertAreaSlot()'s frame is the one to trust -- the _RandomizeAddress() frame
above it has a `this` that does not match its callers', so KDL did not recover
its arguments -- and it is self-consistent:

    start       0x100000     USER_BASE_ANY
    size        0x80000      512 KB
    end         0x7fffffff   fEndAddress
    addressSpec 6            B_RANDOMIZED_ANY_ADDRESS
    alignment   0x1000

addressSpec 6 pins down the call: mmap() in libroot chooses
B_RANDOMIZED_ANY_ADDRESS only for a NULL hint address, and InsertArea() then
takes searchBase from USER_BASE_ANY and searchEnd from fEndAddress, which is
what the frame shows. So this is a plain anonymous-hint mmap of 512 KB -- one
of Chromium's shared memory regions -- in a 32-bit address space.

That rules out four of the six _RandomizeAddress() call sites by inspection:
the one before the search loop needs B_RANDOMIZED_BASE_ADDRESS, and the three
inside the loop are each guarded by is_valid_spot(), which establishes
alignedBase + size - 1 <= limit and therefore start <= end. What is left is
the last resort.

## What happens

When the ordinary search finds no hole, _InsertAreaSlot() falls through to the
reserved areas. For a reserved area that can hold the request at its end, it
picks a randomised base inside the area (around line 765):

    addr_t startRange = next->Base() + next->Size();
    startRange -= size + kMaxRandomize;
    startRange = ROUNDDOWN(startRange, alignment);
    startRange = std::max(startRange, alignedNextBase);

    addr_t rangeEnd
        = std::min(next->Base() + next->Size() - size, end);
    alignedBase = _RandomizeAddress(startRange, rangeEnd, alignment);

The second line is an unguarded subtraction on unsigned addresses, and it is
the only one of the six that computes its own start rather than taking one
is_valid_spot() has already checked. On 32-bit kMaxRandomize is 0x800000 --
8 MB -- so whenever

    next->Base() + next->Size() < size + 8 MB

it underflows and startRange lands near 2^32. ROUNDDOWN keeps it there,
std::max() keeps it there, and _RandomizeAddress() is called with a start far
above its end.

For a 512 KB request that condition is "the reserved area ends below 8.5 MB",
which is an ordinary place for one to be. What makes it rare is only that this
branch is reached after the ordinary search has already failed -- an address
space that is full or badly fragmented, which is what a 32-bit browser
produces after a few hundred mappings. Both panics here came after minutes of
page loading, and both were 512 KB mappings.

## On a release kernel

ASSERT is compiled out below KDEBUG_LEVEL 2, so on a release kernel there is
no panic and no diagnostic. _RandomizeAddress() instead computes

    addr_t range = end - start + 1;

which underflows in turn, giving a range of nearly 2^32, and returns
start + (random % range) -- an arbitrary address, which is then used as the
base of the new area. That is worse than the panic, not better.

## The fix

Do the subtraction only when it cannot underflow. When it would, the clamp on
the next line is the right answer anyway: alignedNextBase is already known to
satisfy alignedNextBase + size - 1 <= min(next->Base() + next->Size() - 1,
end) from the is_valid_spot() test that guards this branch, so it is a valid
start and it is <= rangeEnd.

## Also worth a look, not patched here

The sanity check at the top of _InsertAreaSlot(),

    if (start < fBase || size == 0 || end > fEndAddress
        || start + (size - 1) > end)

overflows for a start near the top of the address space, and it runs before
start is aligned up. Neither could have produced this panic -- the arguments
above are small and page-aligned -- so they are left alone rather than fixed
on speculation.

--- a/src/system/kernel/vm/VMUserAddressSpace.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/vm/VMUserAddressSpace.cpp
@@ -762,8 +762,17 @@
 							addr_t alignedNextBase = align_address(next->Base(),
 								alignment);
 
 							addr_t startRange = next->Base() + next->Size();
-							startRange -= size + kMaxRandomize;
+							// Unsigned, and kMaxRandomize is 8 MB on 32-bit:
+							// a reserved area low in the address space makes
+							// this underflow, and _RandomizeAddress() is then
+							// called with a start above its end. The clamp
+							// below is the right answer in that case --
+							// alignedNextBase is a valid start, by the
+							// is_valid_spot() test that guards this branch.
+							if (startRange < size + kMaxRandomize)
+								startRange = alignedNextBase;
+							else
+								startRange -= size + kMaxRandomize;
 							startRange = ROUNDDOWN(startRange, alignment);
 							startRange = std::max(startRange, alignedNextBase);
 
